[[Johann Sebastian Bach]] composed the [[church cantata]] '''{{lang|de|Lobe den Herren, den mächtigen König der Ehren}}''' (Praise the Lord, the mighty King of honor),{{sfn|Dellal|2025}} '''{{abbr|BWV|Bach-Werke-Verzeichnis (catalogue of Bach's works)}}{{nbsp}}137''', in [[Leipzig]] for the twelfth Sunday after [[Trinity Sunday|Trinity]] and led the first performance on 19 August 1725. The [[Chorale cantata (Bach)|chorale cantata]] is based on [[Lobe den Herren|the hymn]] by [[Joachim Neander]], a general song of praise published in 1680.

Bach composed the cantata in his third year in office as [[Thomaskantor]] in Leipzig. He set the unchanged text of the hymn to music, structuring the cantata in five movements, with choral outer movements framing three intimate [[aria]]s. He scored it for four vocal soloists, a [[SATB|four-part]] choir, and a festive [[Baroque instruments|Baroque instrumental]] ensemble of three trumpets with timpani, two oboes, strings and continuo. The chorale melody is present in all of the movements, in varied treatment.

Bach used the second movement for his [[Schübler Chorales]], and made the closing chorale, which unusually features independent parts for the trumpets and timpani, also part of a 1729 wedding cantata.

== History and words ==
In office as [[Thomaskantor]], director of church music in [[Leipzig]], Bach composed {{lang|de|Lobe den Herren, den mächtigen König der Ehren}} for the [[List of church cantatas by liturgical occasion#12th Sunday after Trinity (Trinity XII)|Twelfth Sunday after Trinity]].{{sfn|''Bach Digital''|2025}}{{sfn|Dürr|Jones|2006|p=[https://books.google.com/books?id=m9JuwslMcq4C&pg=PA505 505]}} It forms part of a [[Chorale cantata cycle|cycle of chorale cantatas]] which Bach composed mainly from mid-1724, his second year in the position. He had composed chorale cantatas between the first Sunday after [[Trinity Sunday|Trinity]] of 1724 and [[Palm Sunday]], but for [[Easter]] had returned to cantatas on more varied texts, possibly because he lost his [[Libretto|librettist]].{{sfn|Wolff|2000}} Later, Bach composed more chorale cantatas to complete the cycle. This cantata is one of the completing works. It is based entirely on the unchanged words on the [[hymn]] "{{langr|de|[[Lobe den Herren, den mächtigen König der Ehren]]}}" (1680) by [[Joachim Neander]].{{sfn|''Bach Digital''|2025}}{{sfn|Dürr|Jones|2006|p=[https://books.google.com/books?id=m9JuwslMcq4C&pg=PA505 505]}}{{sfn|Wolf|2015}}

The prescribed readings for the Sunday were from the [[Second Epistle to the Corinthians]], the ministry of the Spirit ({{Sourcetext|source=Bible|version=King James|book=2 Corinthians|chapter=3|verse=4|range=–11}}), and from the [[Gospel of Mark]], the [[Healing the deaf mute of Decapolis|healing of a deaf mute man]] ({{Sourcetext|source=Bible|version=King James|book=Mark|chapter=7|verse=31|range=–37}}).{{sfn|''Bach Digital''|2025}}{{sfn|Dürr|Jones|2006|p=[https://books.google.com/books?id=m9JuwslMcq4C&pg=PA501 501]}} Unlike most chorale cantatas of the second cycle, but similar to the early {{lang|de|[[Christ lag in Todes Banden, BWV 4|''Christ lag in Todes Banden'', BWV 4]] |italic=unset}}, Bach retained the chorale text unchanged, thus without a reference to the readings.{{sfn|Wolf|2015}}{{sfn|Dürr|Jones|2006|p=[https://books.google.com/books?id=m9JuwslMcq4C&pg=PA505 505]}}

[[John Eliot Gardiner]] assumes, looking at the festive instrumentation and the general content of praise and thanksgiving, that the cantata was also performed that year to celebrate ''Ratswahl'', the inauguration of the town council.{{sfn|Gardiner|2010}} In 1729 Bach used the setting of the final chorale, [[Transposition (music)|transposed]] to [[D major]], to conclude the wedding cantata {{lang|de|[[Herr Gott, Beherrscher aller Dinge, BWV 120a|''Herr Gott, Beherrscher aller Dinge'', BWV 120a]] |italic=unset}}, with the last two [[stanza]]s of the hymn.{{sfn|Mincham|2010}} With its general content of praise, and no specific narrative, the cantata is suitable for many occasions.{{sfn|Wolf|2015}}

== Music ==
=== Structure and scoring ===
Bach structured the cantata into five [[Movement (music)|movements]]. A [[chorale fantasia]] and a closing [[chorale]] frame a sequence of three arias for vocal soloists with accompaniment of {{lang|it|[[obbligato]]}} instruments. Bach scored the work for four vocal soloists ([[soprano]], [[alto]], [[tenor]] and [[Bass (voice type)|bass]]), a [[SATB|four-part choir]] and a festive [[Baroque instruments|Baroque instrumental]] ensemble of three [[trumpets]] (Tr), [[timpani]], two [[oboe]]s (Ob), two [[violin]]s (Vl), [[viola]] (Va), and [[basso continuo]].{{sfn|''Bach Digital''|2025}}{{sfn|Wolf|2015}}{{sfn|Dürr|Jones|2006|pp=[https://books.google.com/books?id=m9JuwslMcq4C&pg=PA504 504–505]}} The duration is given as 18 minutes.{{sfn|Dürr|Jones|2006|p=[https://books.google.com/books?id=m9JuwslMcq4C&pg=PA504 504]}}

In the following table of the movements, the scoring follows the [[Neue Bach-Ausgabe]]. The [[Key (music)|keys]] and [[time signature]]s are taken from [[Alfred Dürr]]'s {{lang|de|Die Kantaten von Johann Sebastian Bach}}.{{sfn|Dürr|Jones|2006|pp=[https://books.google.com/books?id=m9JuwslMcq4C&pg=PA505 504–505]}} The continuo, playing throughout, and the timpani, always playing with the three trumpets, are not shown.

