=== Movements ===
[[File:Lobe den Herren.jpg|thumb|upright=0.9|alt=17th-century print of the five stanzas of a hymn, preceeded by the first line from [[Psalm 103]], with the lines more or less centered |The hymn text in a 1686 print]]
As Dürr and Gardiner observed, the text as well as the chorale melody is present in all movements. The melody in [[bar form]] has an unusual {{lang|de|Stollen}} of five measures{{sfn|Mincham|2010}} and reaches a climax at the beginning of the {{lang|de|Abgesang}}, which Bach also stresses in a variety of means in the movements.{{sfn|Dürr|Jones|2006|p=[https://books.google.com/books?id=m9JuwslMcq4C&pg=PA505 505]}} The cantata is constructed in symmetry: the soprano carries the melody in the outer movements; in the second movement it is sung by the alto, and in the fourth movement played by the trumpet. In the central movement, the beginning of both the vocal and the instrumental theme are derived from it for the cantata's most intimate setting.{{sfn|Dürr|Jones|2006|p=[https://books.google.com/books?id=m9JuwslMcq4C&pg=PA505 505]}}{{sfn|Gardiner|2010}}

==== 1 ====
In the opening chorus, "{{langr|de|Lobe den Herren, den mächtigen König der Ehren}}" (Praise the Lord, the mighty King of honor),{{sfn|Dellal|2025}} the first stanza of the hymn, is rendered as a [[chorale fantasia]]. The trumpets, oboes and strings begin with an instrumental concerto; the soprano sings the {{lang|la|[[cantus firmus]]}} while the lower voices prepare the entries by [[Imitation (music)|imitation]] of the instrumental [[Motif (music)|motifs]].{{sfn|Dürr|Jones|2006|p=[https://books.google.com/books?id=m9JuwslMcq4C&pg=PA505 505]}} For the words "{{langr|de|Kommet zu Hauf, Psalter und Harfen, wacht auf}}" (Come join the crowd, psaltery and harps, awake!),{{sfn|Dellal|2025}} the setting is [[Homophony|homophonic]] and thus singled out.{{sfn|Dürr|Jones|2006|p=[https://books.google.com/books?id=m9JuwslMcq4C&pg=PA505 505]}}{{sfn|Gardiner|2010}}

==== 2 ====
In the second movement, "{{langr|de|Lobe den Herren, der alles so herrlich regieret}}" (Praise the Lord, who directs everything so gloriously),{{sfn|Dellal|2025}} a solo violin accompanies the embellished melody of the chorale. Bach included this movement in his [[Schübler Chorales]], but on a text for [[Advent]], "{{langr|de|Kommst du nun, Jesu, vom Himmel herunter auf Erden}}" ('Come thou, Jesu, from heaven to earth').{{sfn|Dürr|Jones|2006|p=[https://books.google.com/books?id=m9JuwslMcq4C&pg=PA505 505]}}

==== 3 ====
The third stanza, "{{langr|de|Lobe den Herren, der künstlich und fein dich bereitet}}" (Praise the Lord, who prepares you wisely and well),{{sfn|Dellal|2025}} is set as a duet of soprano and bass.{{sfn|Dürr|Jones|2006|pp=[https://books.google.com/books?id=m9JuwslMcq4C&pg=PA505 505–506]}} In great contrast to [[C major]] and [[G major]], the central movement is in [[E minor]].{{sfn|Dürr|Jones|2006|p=[https://books.google.com/books?id=m9JuwslMcq4C&pg=PA504 504]}} Two [[obbligato]] oboes take part in the setting; their motifs are derived from the beginning of the chorale melody.{{sfn|Dürr|Jones|2006|pp=[https://books.google.com/books?id=m9JuwslMcq4C&pg=PA505 505–506]}} In an unusual way, the first vocal section is repeated three times; only the words "{{langr|de|In wieviel Not}}" (in how much suffering){{sfn|Dellal|2025}} are set differently,{{sfn|Dürr|Jones|2006|p=[https://books.google.com/books?id=m9JuwslMcq4C&pg=PA506 506]}} in "grinding [[Chromatic scale|chromatic]] descent".{{sfn|Gardiner|2010}}

==== 4 ====
The fourth movement, "{{langr|de|Lobe den Herren, der deinen Stand sichtbar gesegnet}}" (Praise the Lord, who surely blesses your condition),{{sfn|Dellal|2025}} is in [[A minor]],{{sfn|Dürr|Jones|2006|p=[https://books.google.com/books?id=m9JuwslMcq4C&pg=PA504 504]}} but the cantus firmus of the trumpet is nonetheless in C major,{{sfn|Dürr|Jones|2006|p=[https://books.google.com/books?id=m9JuwslMcq4C&pg=PA506 506]}} in "a battle for harmonic supremacy".{{sfn|Gardiner|2010}} In the final movement of his ''[[Christmas Oratorio]]'' Bach would later embed the chorale in [[Phrygian mode]] in a concerto in D major.{{sfn|Dürr|Jones|2006|p=[https://books.google.com/books?id=m9JuwslMcq4C&pg=PA182 182]}} The independent vocal line quotes parts of the chorale melody several times. The words "{{langr|de|Denke daran}}" (consider this){{sfn|Dellal|2025}} are accented by a different metre, matching the slightly changed words "{{langr|de|Denke dran}}".{{sfn|Dürr|Jones|2006|p=[https://books.google.com/books?id=m9JuwslMcq4C&pg=PA506 506]}}

==== 5 ====
For the closing chorale, Bach set the last hymn stanza, "{{langr|de|Lobe den Herren, was in mir ist, lobe den Namen!}}" (Praise the Lord, what is within me, praise his name!),{{sfn|Dellal|2025}} as four-part vocal setting doubled by the strings and oboes, with independent parts for three trumpets and timpani.{{sfn|Dürr|Jones|2006|p=[https://books.google.com/books?id=m9JuwslMcq4C&pg=PA506 506]}}{{sfn|Dahn|2025}}{{sfn|Hengel|2020}} The first trumpet "[soaring] above all", illustrates the words of the final lines effectively for an affirmative conclusion.{{sfn|Mincham|2010}} Gardiner notes that Bach "knew exactly how best to use the resources of the ceremonial trumpet-led orchestra and choir of his day to convey unbridled joy and majesty".{{sfn|Gardiner|2010}}

